fix: the entry dimension belongs to the project, not to its closure

Every dependency was recorded once per build entry (server/client), so the ledger
claimed two builds for something built once and put an entry name, and an implied
target_type, on packages that know nothing about either. Only the project's own
package has entries now; a dependency is recorded once (entry 'default'), and only
its artifacts live in that record.

The project's record also used to absorb the whole closure, which meant the release
notes row for a dependency could be rendered from the project's record -- levilamina
is compiled with clang-cl, so fmt showed up as a clang-cl build. A record now only
holds the artifacts of its own package (or of its upstream name, which is what
publishes packages/l/levilamina/<version>/manifest.txt for projects that require
the upstream package from their own repository).

Also: commit_and_push syncs onto the remote before pushing and retries, because a
silently failed fetch leaves a stale remote-tracking ref, the push is rejected as
non-fast-forward, and a round ends up half published. The fetches name the branch
and rebase onto FETCH_HEAD, since a checkout made for one generated branch need not
have an origin/<branch> ref at all.
